   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to cost recovery by\nmunicipalities and other recipients from responsible parties and ensures that\nresponsible parties do not benefit from state assistance payments or grants\nmade by the environmental facilities corporation to municipalities and\nrecipients",
    "basePrintNo": "S8282",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8282-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §1167-a, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8282\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to cost recovery by\nmunicipalities and other recipients from responsible parties and ensures\nthat responsible parties do not benefit from state assistance payments\nor grants made by the environmental facilities corporation to munici-\npalities and recipients\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo encourage cost recovery from responsible parties and ensure that\nthose parties do not benefit from state assistance payments/grants made\nby the Environmental Facilities Corporation and unnecessarily burdening\ntaxpayers.  The New York state Environmental Facilities Corporation\n(EFC) is authorized to provide state assistance payments/grants for\neligible municipal water supply projects designed to comply with federal\nand state drinking water goals and standards. For example, the EFC\nrecently announced several million dollars in state assistance\npayments/grants to address the emerging contaminants 1,4-dioxane, PFOS\nand/or PFOA.\n\nFederal and state drinking water goals and standards are often exceeded\ndue to the acts of polluters or other responsible parties.  Munici-\npalities that receive a state assistance payment/grant are not currently\nrequired to reimburse the EFC for the state assistance payment/grant.\nWhen a municipality seeks damages from a party legally responsible for\ncausing contamination of public water supplies and/or damages to water\nsupply facilities, any state assistance payment/grant made to the muni-\ncipality may be considered a collateral source of payment pursuant to\ncivil practice law and rules section 4545 and other laws, and the amount\nof any award recoverable from a responsible party may be reduced by the\namount of such state assistance payment/grant. Thus, it is the responsi-\nble party that ultimately benefits from the state assistance\npayment/grant to the municipality at the cost of the taxpayers of the\nstate of New York.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the public health law by adding a new section 1167-a\nthat provides for the reimbursement of state assistance payments/grants\nmade to a municipality for an eligible project to remove contaminants,\nsuch as 1,4-dioxane, PFOS and/or PFOA, when the municipality takes legal\naction to recover costs of removing the contaminants from its water\nsupplies caused by responsible parties and it receives a payment from a\nresponsible party for such contamination.\n\n- The amount of the reimbursement to the EFC shall be the portion of the\npayment that is in excess of (1) the total cost of the eligible project\nand (2) the expenses incurred pursuing recovery of such costs from\nresponsible parties.\n\n- This Section also provides that, notwithstanding any other provision\nof law, evidence of any grant or state assistance payment from the EFC\nto any municipality or other recipient shall not be admissible in any\naction brought by such municipality or recipient and that the court\n\nshall not reduce the amount of any award against a responsible party in\nwhole or in part because of such state assistance payment/grant.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date (immediately).\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nParts m and r of chapter 57 of the laws of 2017 amended the public\nhealth law by adding sections 1112 and 1113 requiring the Commissioner\nof the Department of Health to promulgate regulations regarding emerging\ncontaminants after recommendations from a new drinking water advisory\ncouncil. These new drinking water regulations have resulted in new and\nsignificant costs for public water suppliers, their customers and the\nstate. Public water suppliers can mitigate these costs through liti-\ngation against parties who are responsible for the contamination of\nsources of water supply.\n\nThe state has also provided and announced significant state assistance\npayments/grants through the EFC to municipalities and recipients to help\npay the costs for eligible projects that provide treatment, so that such\ntreatment can be installed before funds from litigation are available to\nmunicipalities and recipients.\n\nThis bill is intended to prevent responsible parties from benefitting\nfrom such state assistance payments/grants by prohibiting them from\nbeing considered collateral sources and reducing awards that may be\ngranted to municipalities and recipients in such litigation.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
